00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.899
Welcome to this short course on building enterprise-ready agents

00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:04.500
with the NeMo Agent Toolkit,

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:06.799
where you'll learn how to build agentic AI systems

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:08.500
that go beyond prototypes.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.900
Agentic systems that are observable, deployable, and maintainable

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.500
in production environments.

00:00:13.778 --> 00:00:16.579
By the end of this course, you'll have built a complete

00:00:16.579 --> 00:00:19.278
agentic application with observability,

00:00:19.278 --> 00:00:21.278
API deployment, evaluation,

00:00:21.278 --> 00:00:24.379
and a front-end UI connected to a working agent.

00:00:24.405 --> 00:00:26.605
You know how to build a local prototype agent.

00:00:26.605 --> 00:00:27.897
It works with prompts you send

00:00:27.897 --> 00:00:29.405
it, gives you the answers you'd expect

00:00:29.405 --> 00:00:31.504
against the experiments you've run,

00:00:31.504 --> 00:00:34.005
but shipping it to production is where reality hits.

00:00:34.505 --> 00:00:37.204
Whether you built your agent with LangChain, LlamaIndex,

00:00:37.204 --> 00:00:39.405
CrewAI or custom Python code,

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:42.804
getting it into the hands of others reveals the real challenges.

00:00:43.195 --> 00:00:45.795
Also, while there are many great agentic frameworks available,

00:00:46.274 --> 00:00:48.174
they don't all work together seamlessly.

00:00:48.975 --> 00:00:50.875
These are the day two problems.

00:00:51.674 --> 00:00:53.274
Day one is building the agent.

00:00:53.875 --> 00:00:55.475
Day two is everything else.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.729
Integration complexity.

00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:02.429
Managing multi-agent systems built on heterogeneous components becomes increasingly difficult

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:05.730
as agents include arbitrarily nested tools and sub-agents.

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:08.459
Repeatability.

00:01:08.558 --> 00:01:10.659
Agentic systems are non-deterministic.

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:13.058
Without consistency, their value diminishes.

00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:17.159
A small parameter change or new LLM can dramatically affect performance.

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:20.259
It worked on my machine does not work in production.

00:01:20.459 --> 00:01:23.561
Code reuse. Teams implement great agents and tools,

00:01:23.561 --> 00:01:26.861
but sharing across frameworks often means re-implementation.

00:01:27.361 --> 00:01:29.861
This fragmentation leads to developers redoing work

00:01:29.861 --> 00:01:32.561
instead of leveraging collective efforts across the organization.

00:01:32.953 --> 00:01:35.052
Performance and cost.

00:01:35.153 --> 00:01:39.052
Most computation happens in external systems with expensive LLM calls.

00:01:39.552 --> 00:01:41.552
Bottlenecks hide in the complexity.

00:01:41.753 --> 00:01:45.153
Where's the time being spent? Where are the tokens being consumed?

00:01:45.653 --> 00:01:49.477
Mixed workloads make it difficult to optimize. production requirements.

00:01:49.878 --> 00:01:52.477
We need to expose our agents as API's.

00:01:52.778 --> 00:01:54.778
We need to monitor what's happening inside.

00:01:54.977 --> 00:01:57.677
We have to make sure that edge cases don't break production.

00:01:57.778 --> 00:02:00.078
We need to enable continuous learning from feedback

00:02:00.177 --> 00:02:01.778
and preserve data privacy.

00:02:01.977 --> 00:02:04.778
And we need to build an evaluation to quickly identify issues.

00:02:05.177 --> 00:02:09.445
How do we do these things? NeMo Agent Toolkit is specifically designed

00:02:09.445 --> 00:02:11.295
to address these Day 2 Problems. And it

00:02:11.295 --> 00:02:13.145
works with whatever you built on Day 1.

00:02:13.846 --> 00:02:16.846
The NeMo Agent Toolkit is an open-source Python library

00:02:17.246 --> 00:02:19.445
and it bridges the gap between your prototype agents

00:02:19.445 --> 00:02:21.520
and battle-hardened deployable products.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.620
Being open source means no vendor lock-in.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.319
You can inspect the code, you can contribute improvements,

00:02:26.419 --> 00:02:28.319
and you can deploy anywhere.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.319
You don't have to rip out your existing agent framework

00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:34.120
or rewrite your application to use the NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:02:34.419 --> 00:02:37.319
It works with agents built in any popular framework,

00:02:37.419 --> 00:02:41.319
like LangChain or LangGraph or CrewAI or Semantic Kernel

00:02:41.419 --> 00:02:43.719
or Google ADK or LlamaIndex or whatever you're using.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.020
It augments what you've already built.

00:02:46.209 --> 00:02:48.519
Think of it as a unified interface layer

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:52.528
providing the following. Production infrastructure.

00:02:52.729 --> 00:02:56.739
It provides an API deployment and it's configurable using YAML.

00:02:57.139 --> 00:03:00.419
This allows for rapid prototyping and quick changes.

00:03:00.675 --> 00:03:02.775
Unified observability.

00:03:02.775 --> 00:03:05.675
It provides end-to-end tracing across heterogeneous frameworks.

00:03:05.675 --> 00:03:08.175
You can see exactly what's happening,

00:03:08.175 --> 00:03:10.474
even when agents call other agents in different frameworks.

00:03:11.137 --> 00:03:13.236
Systematic evaluation.

00:03:13.637 --> 00:03:16.937
It provides standardized, fully customizable evaluations

00:03:17.037 --> 00:03:19.137
for any part of your workflow.

00:03:19.637 --> 00:03:22.437
Performance intelligence. Find bottlenecks,

00:03:22.537 --> 00:03:26.736
profile your workflow, and discover optimization opportunities.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.000
Automatic hyperparameter tuning will allow you to improve accuracy and reduce costs.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.199
Integration support. As we'll see, the NeMo Agent Toolkit supports many plugins,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.000
including memory systems, MCP, including client and server, and more.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.800
Installation is straightforward, just pip install nvidia-nat.

00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:49.000
And there are optional plugins for specific

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.800
frameworks that you can install as needed.

00:03:51.152 --> 00:03:54.052
Here's where the NeMo Agent Toolkit differs from a typical library.

00:03:54.152 --> 00:03:55.752
It's config driven.

00:03:55.953 --> 00:03:59.353
Instead of hard-coding agents and tools and workflows into your code,

00:03:59.552 --> 00:04:02.052
you define them in a YAML configuration file.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.300
Your tools become composable functions.

00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:07.100
Your LLM choices are declared in the config

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.251
and your workflow structure is defined in

00:04:09.251 --> 00:04:11.401
the same config. Why does this matter?

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.401
Because configuration files are easier to change, they can be version controlled

00:04:16.401 --> 00:04:17.739
and you can experiment with them

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:19.300
more easily than you can with code.

00:04:19.714 --> 00:04:22.514
You can swap an LLM without even touching your Python.

00:04:23.014 --> 00:04:24.414
You can add a new tool by

00:04:24.414 --> 00:04:26.014
adding a few lines to your YAML config.

00:04:26.615 --> 00:04:29.014
You can run evaluations against different workflow configurations

00:04:29.014 --> 00:04:31.514
to see what works best, all without touching your code.

00:04:32.014 --> 00:04:34.214
Throughout this course, you're going to see this in action

00:04:34.214 --> 00:04:36.014
as you build a climate science chatbot.

00:04:36.514 --> 00:04:39.314
But the patterns we'll see would apply to any agentic system.

00:04:39.915 --> 00:04:44.146
When you move agents to production, several concrete problems emerge.

00:04:44.745 --> 00:04:47.315
First, visibility into what's happening.

00:04:47.915 --> 00:04:50.016
Your agent takes an input and returns an output,

00:04:50.315 --> 00:04:53.502
but what happened in between? How many tools were called?

00:04:53.802 --> 00:04:55.603
What order were those tools called in?

00:04:55.802 --> 00:04:59.202
If there was an error, what was the error? Without this visibility,

00:04:59.403 --> 00:05:01.702
debugging production issues is guesswork.

00:05:02.103 --> 00:05:03.903
Second, deploying as a service.

00:05:04.165 --> 00:05:06.735
A local script is not useful to other applications.

00:05:07.035 --> 00:05:10.035
You have to expose your agent as an API of some sort,

00:05:10.334 --> 00:05:13.735
so other services and dashboards and applications can query it,

00:05:14.035 --> 00:05:16.735
and this requires a structured deployment. Third,

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:18.889
knowing if it's working.

00:05:18.899 --> 00:05:20.889
Your agent seemed correct during testing,

00:05:21.088 --> 00:05:23.189
but after deployment you'll discover edge cases

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.689
or hallucinations or incorrect tool choices,

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:27.889
and you have to have systematic evaluation

00:05:27.988 --> 00:05:29.788
to catch these before they affect users.

00:05:30.137 --> 00:05:32.737
Fourth, understanding performance and cost.

00:05:33.237 --> 00:05:36.836
LLM calls cost money, and tool invocations take time.

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:39.937
So in production, you need granular insight

00:05:39.937 --> 00:05:41.506
into where your cycles and dollars

00:05:41.506 --> 00:05:43.336
are going so you can intelligently optimize.

00:05:43.637 --> 00:05:45.437
The NeMo Agent Toolkit provides concrete

00:05:45.437 --> 00:05:47.536
tools to solve each of these problems.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.661
How do you get telemetry and performance data from your agentic system?

00:05:52.161 --> 00:05:55.060
Some teams writing agentic code just don't think about this.

00:05:55.661 --> 00:05:59.961
Other teams hard code the telemetry sinks within their code base.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.961
So their logic is interweaved with logging and telemetry sinks.

00:06:04.461 --> 00:06:07.560
But a better way would be to instrument your code base

00:06:07.761 --> 00:06:10.411
with something that can take OpenTelemetry data and

00:06:10.411 --> 00:06:13.060
pass it on to your observability team system.

00:06:13.461 --> 00:06:15.934
The NeMo Agent Toolkit makes it possible

00:06:15.934 --> 00:06:18.761
to add this instrumentation via a config file

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.461
versus interweaving your code's logic with telemetry sinks.

00:06:22.788 --> 00:06:25.678
The result is a unified single pane of glass view

00:06:25.678 --> 00:06:27.588
that provides complete visibility

00:06:27.588 --> 00:06:30.588
and it empowers your team to manage and debug complex systems

00:06:30.588 --> 00:06:32.689
with the tools they already know and trust.

00:06:32.988 --> 00:06:36.208
The benefit here, when something goes wrong in production,

00:06:36.307 --> 00:06:38.807
you have visibility into the execution trace.

00:06:39.208 --> 00:06:41.408
You can identify whether the issue is

00:06:41.408 --> 00:06:43.608
a slow tool or excessive token usage,

00:06:43.708 --> 00:06:46.408
or an incorrect tool selection or something else entirely.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.040
We just talked about observability.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:54.040
The NeMo Agent Toolkit also provides evaluation, which is similar but different.

00:06:54.641 --> 00:06:56.394
Observability tells you what happened, it

00:06:56.394 --> 00:06:58.440
lets you look into the running system,

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.540
but evaluation tells you whether what happened was correct,

00:07:01.807 --> 00:07:05.608
and you know what is correct or incorrect for your agentic system.

00:07:06.108 --> 00:07:09.608
Given an input, you know what the correct output should be.

00:07:10.108 --> 00:07:13.608
The NeMo Agent Toolkit allows you to build an evaluation set

00:07:14.307 --> 00:07:16.108
of these inputs and outputs

00:07:16.507 --> 00:07:20.007
and run them in an automated way to test your agentic system.

00:07:20.367 --> 00:07:23.567
Then you're free to make changes to your configuration file

00:07:23.867 --> 00:07:27.168
and run the evals to see how it affected your agent.

00:07:27.867 --> 00:07:30.267
This matters because agents are adaptive systems.

00:07:30.367 --> 00:07:32.461
They don't follow predetermined code paths.

00:07:32.461 --> 00:07:34.961
The reason they adapt, and this is powerful,

00:07:34.961 --> 00:07:37.360
but it also means that edge cases can pop up.

00:07:37.761 --> 00:07:40.360
And evaluation helps you discover these systematically.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.961
During this course, you'll actually encounter a bug that emerges after deployment.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.560
Something that wouldn't show up in casual testing.

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.471
and you'll use evaluation to catch it systematically,

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.771
and then you'll understand why it happened.

00:07:53.471 --> 00:07:55.571
Moving beyond visibility and evaluation,

00:07:55.771 --> 00:07:59.971
the NeMo Agent Toolkit includes automatic hyperparameter optimization

00:08:00.471 --> 00:08:02.771
and comprehensive profiling capabilities.

00:08:03.471 --> 00:08:06.971
This slide shows an agentic workflow that we were able to optimize.

00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:11.386
Each chart is a series of agentic iterations over time.

00:08:12.156 --> 00:08:14.069
On the left, you can see that

00:08:14.069 --> 00:08:16.255
we had a bunch of sequential tool calls

00:08:16.786 --> 00:08:18.253
and the optimizer was able to

00:08:18.253 --> 00:08:19.966
show us that these could be parallelized.

00:08:20.516 --> 00:08:23.886
So on the right, you see after we parallelized those tool calls,

00:08:23.886 --> 00:08:25.485
we were able to dramatically reduce the

00:08:25.485 --> 00:08:27.086
amount of time the agentic workflow took.

00:08:27.432 --> 00:08:30.632
The optimizer uses Optuna and genetic algorithms

00:08:30.632 --> 00:08:33.231
to automatically tune your workflows.

00:08:33.932 --> 00:08:35.932
In one agentic application,

00:08:35.932 --> 00:08:38.932
we saw massive improvements just by running the optimizer.

00:08:39.317 --> 00:08:41.518
So how does the optimizer work?

00:08:41.817 --> 00:08:43.817
You define which parameters are tunable,

00:08:43.817 --> 00:08:46.118
like the LLM temperature or the model choice

00:08:46.118 --> 00:08:48.618
or retry logic or tool parameters.

00:08:49.118 --> 00:08:51.118
And then you say which metrics matter to you

00:08:51.118 --> 00:08:53.317
like accuracy or latency or token cost.

00:08:53.618 --> 00:08:56.317
And then the optimizer runs your workflows against test cases

00:08:56.317 --> 00:08:59.118
with different combinations of parameters,

00:08:59.118 --> 00:09:01.618
and it finds the settings that optimize for your goals.

00:09:02.033 --> 00:09:05.333
The profiler gives you insight into workflow performance.

00:09:05.833 --> 00:09:08.533
On this slide, we can see an agentic workflow

00:09:08.583 --> 00:09:10.634
that is tested across many different models

00:09:10.833 --> 00:09:12.634
with input or prompt tokens

00:09:12.734 --> 00:09:14.833
and output or completion tokens.

00:09:15.192 --> 00:09:17.213
And the profiler lets us see how

00:09:17.213 --> 00:09:19.523
those different models interact with the agentic workflow.

00:09:19.993 --> 00:09:23.072
The profiler gives detailed insights into token usage,

00:09:23.452 --> 00:09:25.493
tool execution times, workflow patterns.

00:09:25.993 --> 00:09:28.192
You need these to be able to optimize your agent.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:31.389
And together these tools eliminate the guesswork from agent tuning.

00:09:31.889 --> 00:09:34.720
We won't implement these in the course, but they're powerful capabilities

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.940
you can explore after mastering the fundamentals.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.350
The NeMo Agent Toolkit lets you compose your various systems together.

00:09:42.259 --> 00:09:46.019
It makes your agents and tools reusable mix and match components.

00:09:46.289 --> 00:09:49.154
And here we can see some of the

00:09:49.154 --> 00:09:52.019
frameworks and libraries that it integrates with natively.

00:09:52.197 --> 00:09:53.767
It's open source and pluggable, so

00:09:53.767 --> 00:09:55.597
if you don't see your library here,

00:09:55.798 --> 00:09:57.571
you can easily integrate it and we're

00:09:57.571 --> 00:09:59.597
adding new frameworks and libraries all the time.

00:10:00.097 --> 00:10:01.998
You're going to build a real system.

00:10:02.398 --> 00:10:04.298
This will be a climate science chatbot

00:10:04.298 --> 00:10:08.597
capable of fetching and analyzing and visualizing real NOAA climate data.

00:10:08.898 --> 00:10:10.798
And it's not going to just be a demo.

00:10:11.097 --> 00:10:13.298
You're going to write the code, you're going to deploy it yourself,

00:10:13.597 --> 00:10:16.798
and you'll see it work and sometimes fail in real time.

00:10:16.998 --> 00:10:18.798
You'll face real challenges.

00:10:19.243 --> 00:10:22.043
connecting to APIs, composing multiple agents,

00:10:22.143 --> 00:10:24.043
debugging unexpected behavior.

00:10:24.442 --> 00:10:26.243
We're going to observe token usage

00:10:26.342 --> 00:10:28.442
and then we're going to deploy it for actual queries.

00:10:28.842 --> 00:10:32.143
And you'll even discover and fix a real bug using evaluation tools.

00:10:32.442 --> 00:10:36.043
You'll start with a basic ReAct agent built from standalone Python functions.

00:10:36.442 --> 00:10:38.743
This will be a concrete, simple foundation,

00:10:38.842 --> 00:10:41.143
and then you'll progressively add real-world capabilities.

00:10:41.442 --> 00:10:43.743
API deployment, observability integration,

00:10:43.842 --> 00:10:46.543
interoperability, systematic evaluation,

00:10:46.743 --> 00:10:50.543
and finally a UI. Each step builds directly on the previous one.

00:10:51.048 --> 00:10:53.149
you'll learn production patterns

00:10:53.149 --> 00:10:55.249
by implementing them with your own hands.
